## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a real-time, compliant ML inference workflow using AWS services and to articulate trade-offs between latency, cost, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- Serverless streaming (Kinesis Data Streams)
- SageMaker inference endpoints
- DynamoDB with PK and GSI
- Glue Data Catalog and schema evolution
- Privacy controls (KMS, VPC endpoints, encryption in transit, IAM)
- Reliability patterns (DLQ, idempotent processing)
- Cost optimization (batch inferences, autoscaling)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lambda snippet calling SageMaker endpoint
const aws = require('aws-sdk');
const Sage = new aws.SageMakerRuntime();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const payload = event.records.map(r => r.data).join('\n');
  const res = await Sage.invokeEndpoint({ EndpointName: 'sentiment-intent', ContentType: 'text/plain', Body: payload }).promise();
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate end-to-end latency and implement backpressure?
- How would you handle schema evolution in Glue when new fields appear in transcripts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with Rekognition Video, including asynchronous results and integration with DynamoDB and S3. It also exercises privacy, error handling, and cost trade-offs in a beginner-friendly AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Rekognition Video asynchronous APIs (moderation, labels)
- SNS/SQS notifications and S3 event triggers
- Lambda orchestration and retries
- DynamoDB upsert patterns and compact metadata design
- IAM least privilege and KMS for encryption
- Privacy, retention, and cost considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// process Rekognition output and update DynamoDB
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
async function upsertVideoIndex(videoId, s3Key, labels, status){
  const item = { videoId, s3Key, labelList: labels, moderationStatus: status, timestamp: Date.now() };
  await ddb.put({TableName:'VideoIndex', Item:item}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 1000+ concurrent videos and handle partial failures?
- What changes would you make to support different video formats and longer durations while controlling costs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end, scalable, privacy-conscious moderation pipeline across languages, with proper data governance and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture, cross-language moderation, PII redaction, data residency, IAM least privilege, audit logs.
- Use of Kinesis, S3, SageMaker/Comprehend, Translate, and DynamoDB with proper artifact keys and provenance.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: event-driven steps
function processMessage(evt){
  const msg = ingest(evt);
  const moderation = callModeration(msg);
  const translated = translateIfNeeded(moderation);
  const redacted = redactPII(translated);
  const summary = summarize(redacted);
  storeArtifacts(msg, redacted, summary);
  upsertIndex(msg, redacted, summary);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure idempotent processing and prevent duplicate moderation scores?
- How would you test redaction accuracy and model drift over time?

